WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:05.049
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:10.300
Creating geometry for a computer game or a
movie is a very long and arduous task.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:15.160
For instance, if we would like to populate
a virtual city with buildings, it would cost

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.579
a ton of time and money and of course, we
would need quite a few artists.

00:00:19.579 --> 00:00:24.749
This piece of work solves this problem in
a very elegant and convenient way: it learns

00:00:24.749 --> 00:00:30.480
the preference of the user, then creates and
recommends a set of solutions that are expected

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.949
to be desirable.

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:37.579
In this example, we are looking for tables
with either one leg or crossing legs.

00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:43.510
It should also be properly balanced, therefore
if we see any of these criteria, we'll assign

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:46.219
a high score to these models.

00:00:46.219 --> 00:00:55.940
These are the preferences that the algorithm
should try to learn.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:01.660
The orange bars show the predicted score for
new models created by the algorithm - a larger

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:09.040
value means that the system expects the user
to score these high, and the blue bars mean

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.040
the uncertainty.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.090
Generally, we're looking for solutions with
large orange and small blue bars, this means

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:19.310
that the algorithm is confident that a given
model is in line with our preferences.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:26.009
And we see exactly what were looking for - novel,
balanced table designs with one leg or crossed

00:01:26.009 --> 00:01:27.210
legs.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:34.750
Interestingly, since we have these uncertainty
values, one can also visualize counterexamples

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:40.030
where the algorithm is not so sure, but would
guess that we wouldn't like the model.

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:43.990
It's super cool that it is aware how horrendous
these designs looks.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:47.869
It may have a better eye than many of the
contemporary art curators out there.

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:55.560
There are also examples where the algorithm
is very confident that we're going to hate

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:01.900
a given example because of its legs or unbalancedness,
and would never recommend such a model.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:07.570
So indirectly, it also learns how a balanced
piece of furniture should look like, without

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:13.110
ever learning the concept of gravity or doing
any kind of architectural computation.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:18.319
The algorithm also works on buildings, and
after learning our preferences, it can populate

00:02:18.319 --> 00:02:25.910
entire cities with geometry that is in line
with our artistic vision.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:29.520
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

